## 4. Fit a simple regression

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Fit and identify this foundational simple-regression model independently; AI-generated code or commands are not permitted for this item.</p>

Fit:

\[
total\_rentals_i = b_0 + b_1 temperature\_c_i + e_i.
\]

Print the model summary and save the fitted model as `bike_model`.

```{r}
# Fit and inspect bike_model here.
bike_model <- lm(total_rentals ~ temperature_c, data = bikes)
summary(bike_model)
```

## 5. Interpret slope and intercept

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Interpret both coefficients and assess the intercept's usefulness using your own reasoning.</p>

Interpret the slope in daily rentals per degree Celsius using "associated with" and "on average." Then interpret the intercept. Check the observed temperature range and explain whether the zero-degree intercept is substantively useful or primarily a line-positioning quantity.

**The slope coefficient is 161.968, which means that for an increase of 1 degree celsius in temperature, this will be associated with a 161.968 increase of rentals daily on average. The intercept coefficient was 1214.639, which means that at a temperature of 0 degrees celsius, the amount of rentals is expected to be 1214.639. Because the lowest observed temperature was around 2, this intercept is not really useful.**

## 6. Fitted value and residual

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-yellow">YELLOW</span> AI may assist with `mutate()`, `fitted()`, or `residuals()` syntax or debugging; verify the selected date and numerical outputs.</p>
<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Explain the residual sign and the observed = fitted + residual identity independently.</p>

Create `bikes_augmented` from `bikes` using `mutate()`, `fitted(bike_model)`, and `residuals(bike_model)`. Name the new columns `fitted` and `resid`. For **2012-05-15**:

- report observed rentals;
- report fitted rentals;
- report the residual;
- explain the residual's sign in words;
- verify numerically that observed = fitted + residual.

```{r}
# Add fitted values/residuals and inspect 2012-05-15.
bikes_augmented <- bikes |>
  mutate(
    fitted = fitted(bike_model),
    residual  = residuals(bike_model)
  )

bikes_augmented |>
  filter(date == as.Date("2012-05-15")) |>
  select(date, total_rentals, fitted, residual)
```

**For 5/15/2012, the observed rentals was 5115, the fitted rentals is 5276.797 and the residual was -161.7966. This being negative means that the model overpredicted how many rentals would happen on this day based on temperature by about 161. Also, 5276.797 - 161.797 is 5115 so the observed = fitted + residual.**

