## Exam-Style Practice

The following paper-friendly example is independent of the bike-rental analysis. A food-processing plant records one observation per operating day. The outcome is daily electricity use in MWh; the predictor is scheduled production hours. Across observed days, production hours range from 8 to 24.

The fitted simple regression is:

\[
electricity use = 18.00 + 0.75(production\ hours).
\]

| Term | Estimate | Standard error | 95% confidence interval | p-value |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Intercept | 18.00 | 3.00 | [12.10, 23.90] | <0.001 |
| Production hours | 0.75 | 0.20 | [0.35, 1.15] | <0.001 |

On one 20-hour day, actual electricity use was 35 MWh. The sample mean was 30 MWh.

### Block A — Coefficients, fitted value, and residual

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Without R or AI: (a) interpret the slope with units and associational language; (b) interpret the intercept and explain whether it is interpolation or extrapolation; (c) calculate the fitted value for the 20-hour day; and (d) calculate and interpret its residual.</p>

**The slope for this model is 0.75, which in context means that for an increase of 1 production hour, the electricity use is estimated to go up 0.75MWh on average. The intercept is 18 meaning that when there is no production hours the estimated electricity use would be 18MWh, and this is extrapolation because no day actually had 0 production hours. If there was 20 hours, the fitted value is 18+0.75*20 = 33MWh, which gives its residual as 35-33=2, which means the model underpredicted the electricity use for this day by 2MWh.**

### Block B — Uncertainty and claim audit

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Without R or AI: (a) interpret the slope standard error; (b) interpret the 95% confidence interval; (c) state the null tested by the slope p-value and what the reported evidence means; and (d) explain why the output does not establish that adding one scheduled production hour causes electricity use to rise by exactly 0.75 MWh.</p>

**The slope standard error is 0.2, which in context we can see that it is relatively large because it is over a fourth of the size of the slope estimate, and 1 standard deviation would give this slope a 0.2 range. The 95% CI is (0.35,1.15) which means that for a 1 increase in production hours we estimate with 95% confidence the electricity use would go up between 0.35 and 1.15MWh on average. For slope, the null hypothesis is that it is zero, and since the p value is super small we reject this and say there is a meaninfgul positive slope (since the est was 0.75). The 0.75 estimate is an average, meaning for an increase of 1 production hour we expect 0.75 on average increase, but each one is not 0.75 (for example it could be something like 0.7 and 0.8).**

### Block C — Statistical versus practical importance

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Without R or AI: (a) calculate the fitted electricity-use difference for an 8-hour production difference; (b) compare it with the 30-MWh sample mean; (c) discuss statistical and practical importance separately; and (d) name one plausible day-level alternative explanation for the association.</p>

**We can do this by finding the electricity use at 8 hours and subtracting its use at 0 hours, which is 18+0.75*8 - 18+0.75*0 = 18+6-18 = 6MWh difference. With a sample mean of 30, this difference is relatively meaningful especially because there is a total of 16 hours of range. A variable that could influence this outcome is size of plant, because for a bigger plant maybe the one obvservation would be more electricity using on average even if the hours observed was the same.**

### Verify the arithmetic

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-yellow">YELLOW</span> Use the supplied coefficients to verify the fitted value, residual, and eight-hour comparison in R. AI may assist with arithmetic-code syntax, but the interpretations above must be completed independently.</p>

```{r}
